Cisco published CVE-2026-20182 on 2026-05-14, an authentication bypass in the peering mechanism of the Catalyst SD-WAN control plane (CVSS 10.0) that affects SD-WAN Controller, Manager and Validator regardless of device configuration; there is no workaround, and Cisco said it was aware of limited exploitation in May 2026 ([Cisco, 2026-05-14](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-sdwan-rpa2-v69WY2SW)). CISA added it to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog the same day ([CISA KEV catalog, 2026-05-14](https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/feeds/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json)); the catalog entry refers to Emergency Directive 26-03, which CISA first issued on 2026-02-25 for earlier SD-WAN flaws ([CISA, 2026-02-25](https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/directives/ed-26-03-mitigate-vulnerabilities-cisco-sd-wan-systems)). Rapid7, which found the flaw, shows an unauthenticated peer completing the control-plane handshake by presenting itself as a vHub device, injecting an attacker-controlled public key into the `vmanage-admin` account's authorized SSH keys and logging in to the NETCONF service over SSH on TCP port 830 ([Rapid7, 2026-05-14](https://www.rapid7.com/blog/post/ve-cve-2026-20182-critical-authentication-bypass-cisco-catalyst-sd-wan-controller-fixed/)). Cisco Talos clusters the exploitation under UAT-8616 with high confidence: the actor attempted to add SSH keys, modify NETCONF configurations and escalate to root ([Cisco Talos, 2026-05-14](https://blog.talosintelligence.com/sd-wan-ongoing-exploitation/)). Talos also describes separate actors, distinct from UAT-8616, exploiting the earlier SD-WAN flaws CVE-2026-20133, CVE-2026-20128 and CVE-2026-20122 since March 2026 ([Cisco Talos, 2026-05-14](https://blog.talosintelligence.com/sd-wan-ongoing-exploitation/)).

**Exposure:** every Catalyst SD-WAN Controller, Manager and Validator below the first fixed release of its train, whatever its configuration ([Cisco, 2026-05-14](https://sec.cloudapps.cisco.com/security/center/content/CiscoSecurityAdvisory/cisco-sa-sdwan-rpa2-v69WY2SW)).

**Detection:** control-plane peering events from devices that are not in the inventory, new keys in the `vmanage-admin` account's authorized SSH keys, NETCONF sessions over SSH on TCP port 830 from unexpected sources, and configuration changes nobody made ([Rapid7, 2026-05-14](https://www.rapid7.com/blog/post/ve-cve-2026-20182-critical-authentication-bypass-cisco-catalyst-sd-wan-controller-fixed/); [Cisco Talos, 2026-05-14](https://blog.talosintelligence.com/sd-wan-ongoing-exploitation/)).

**Defender takeaway:** an upgrade does not remove a key or configuration an attacker already planted, so on any controller that ran an affected release, check the authorized keys, peers and NETCONF configuration against a known-good state.
